From 4eb1dd7936115d7e8b0cf515136aa60796b98cbc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Church <200545962+BrandonChurch93@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 21:45:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The table's dressing takes the frame's one hash to a pixel
 from eight samples on

At the front of a dressing that comes (spring's petals at 680, and the three
changes after it) a pixel was held against a hash drawn afresh at each sample,
so the number of samples in which it held the dressing fell by chance and the
front stood as a fringe of grain in the film's final frames. From PASS_EVEN
samples on the dressing now reads the frame's one hash to a pixel at its even
levels, stepped with the sample, as the base's pass does.

What this does and does not do. Where the front stands through an exposure,
pixels at one place of it hold the dressing in the same number of samples. In
the film the front runs on while the shutter is open, so the numbers at one
place are near and not the same: at the film's two paces two to six samples
apart at most, their mean square under what chance left at every count and a
tenth to three fifths of it from 24 samples on. One share for the whole
exposure, as a base has, would make them the same and is not written.

Under PASS_EVEN samples the line is not read: a frame of the draft tier and the
page's own frame are what they were. A base and a part inside the glass run
the lines they ran.

tests/shake-change.test.ts restates the test of the exact text (the old rule
was one line for the dressing, a fresh hash at every count) and adds a test of
the numbers held, for a front that stands and for one that passes.

Co-authored-by: Perplexity <noreply@perplexity.ai>
